fb: answer SwapWait at submit in scanout mode, and never block a whole panel frame on the latch

Two measured serial waits made the UI feel stuck even with copy=0 (33 ms per frame at ~30 fps,
40-60 ms end to end):

1. swapWaitInner() waited 1.5 panel frames per swap (9.9 ms). The scanned surface is held in
   fZcHeld until a later frame latches, so CoreAnimation cannot draw into it again: answering at
   submit is safe. sweetfb_swapsync=1 still asks for the honest answer.
2. sc_flush_idle() waited up to 25 ms for the previous CTL flush (16.2 ms per swap). The flush is
   a one-shot latch: the registers written next are what the pending latch takes, so the newest
   frame replaces the queued one. Wait 2 ms at most and count replacements instead of blocking.

--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -838,7 +838,16 @@
 				if (fScanCopy) {
 					IOLog("SWEET FB: scan-out copy on (0235): frames are copied to three framebuffer-owned buffers, a swap is complete when copied\n");
 				}
-				PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_swapsync", &ss, sizeof(ss));
+				const bool ssGiven = PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_swapsync", &ss, sizeof(ss));
+				/*
+				 * 0271: in the GPU scanout mode answer SwapWait at submit. The surface is held in
+				 * fZcHeld until the pipe has latched a later frame, so CoreAnimation cannot draw into
+				 * it again; waiting 1.5 panel frames (measured: 9.9 ms per swap) adds latency for
+				 * nothing. sweetfb_swapsync=1 still asks for the honest answer.
+				 */
+				if (fScanout && !ssGiven) {
+					ss = 0;
+				}
 				PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_vsyncalign", &va, sizeof(va));
 				int lc = 1, nr = 0, lg = 0, vs = 0;
 				PE_parse_boot_argn("sweetfb_vsyncsmooth", &vs, sizeof(vs));
